  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1203 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nDrier conditions are expected for today as a brief period of upper-\nlevel ridging develops behind Saturday's storm system. Despite the \nincreased subsidence, a combination of morning fog, high clouds, and \na Denver Cyclone will aid in keeping cooler temperatures in place \nacross much of the forecast area. Those on the southern side of the \ncyclone may see temperatures in the mid to upper 40s thanks to \ndownsloping effects off the Palmer Divide, whereas those on its \nnorthern extent should expect upper 30s to low 40s where northerly \nwinds will keep cooler air sliding south. Patchy freezing fog is \nexpected to begin developing over the eastern plains late tonight, \nwith areas of dense fog likely setting in just before sunrise. Hi-\nres guidance continues to shift the corridor of thickest fog \nslightly with each run, but has continued to keep its southern most \nedge just making it into the airfield at DIA and northward along the \nI-25 corridor into Larimer County, as well as east along I-70 into \nLincoln County. Anyone driving across the plains in the morning \nshould be prepared to encounter patchy fog, with areas of \nsignificantly reduced visibilities to a quarter mile or less at \ntimes. Additionally, freezing fog can create slick surfaces which \ncould lead to slippery driving conditions on top of lowered \nvisibilities. A Dense Fog Advisory has been issued for Washington \nCounty from 1AM to 10AM, and for the northern I-25 Corridor and \nadjacent plains from Denver northward, areas along Highway 34, and I-\n70 east of Denver from 4AM to 10AM. Conditions should start to \nimprove by late morning. \n\nSynoptically, things look in decent agreement across ensemble suites \nand deterministic outputs alike, with regards to the next \napproaching shortwave expected to move across the Rockies on Monday. \nFor Colorado, it looks like the axis of the upper-level ridge will \npush east by Monday morning, leaving southwesterly flow aloft in its \nwake. Afternoon high temps are forecast to remain five to ten \ndegrees below normal across the lower elevations, with our northern \nmountain valleys staying right around seasonal values. Cross \nsections show a deepening moisture profile throughout the day, with \nsnow showers likely developing in the mountains by the afternoon. \nMoisture still looks unimpressive with this next wave, but despite \nour lower elevations remaining dry, we should at least see some \nlight mountain snowfall accumulations overnight into Tuesday \nmorning (generally two inches or less), with low chances for some \nvery light accumulations for portions of the Palmer Divide. \n\nUpper-level ridging will rebuild across the Rockies behind Monday's \ndisturbance. Afternoon temperatures will gradually warm each day \nthrough the week, with mild and dry conditions looking likely for \nThursday's holiday celebrations. There are enough uncertainties with \nthe the evolution of our next potential storm system to not \ncompletely rule out light mountain snow showers through the week, \nbut our lower elevations are on track to remain dry through the \nforecast period.",
